---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Comments from '../../../components/Comments.astro';

---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Courage to Go Where He Calls: Liberia and Guinea - Caleb McGennis"
  description="Sixteen of us drove from Liberia into Guinea to record Bible stories. We got turned around at a border, dug trucks out of the mud, and I got to record stories in the Guinean Konon language."
  type="article"
  publishedTime="2026-11-20"
  author="Caleb Michael McGennis"
  image="/images/social/liberia-guinea-mission-trip-og.jpg"
>
  <!-- Hero Section -->
  <section class="bg-soho-white py-20 md:py-32 px-4 border-b border-gray-200">
    <div class="container mx-auto max-w-4xl">
      <a href="/blog/entrepreneur-ai" class="inline-flex items-center text-mcgennis-navy hover:text-mcgennis-gold transition-colors mb-8">
        <svg class="w-5 h-5 mr-2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7"/>
        </svg>
        Back to An Entrepreneur in a World of AI
      </a>
      <h1 class="text-5xl md:text-6xl font-display font-bold mb-8 text-gray-900 leading-tight tracking-tight">
        The Courage to Go Where He Calls
      </h1>
      <div class="h-1 w-24 bg-brass-horizontal rounded-full shadow-sm mb-8"></div>
      <p class="text-2xl text-gray-500 font-light mb-8">Liberia, Guinea, sixteen travelers, and Bible stories recorded in Guinean French, Liberian English, Guinean Konon, and Pulaar.</p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-gray-500">
        <span>By Caleb Michael McGennis</span>
        <span>&bull;</span>
        <time datetime="2026-11-20">November 20, 2026</time>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Article Content -->
  <article class="py-16 px-4">
    <div class="container mx-auto max-w-3xl prose prose-lg">
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8 font-light">
        I just got back from West Africa. Ten of us flew into Liberia. Sixteen of us traveled north across the border into Guinea and spent the week recording Bible stories in local languages so people can hear them in their own words.
      </p>

      <figure class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm not-prose">
        <img loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" src="/images/liberia/team-before-the-trip.webp" alt="Ten people standing together on the grass in front of a brick building" class="w-full" />
        <figcaption class="p-4 bg-soho-white text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">The ten of us on November 7, the day we left</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">Welcome to Liberia</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        It started on the plane. I sat near a young missionary on her way to her own next step. She's doing a residency through her church: a year of local missions and teaching language classes, then a placement overseas. It was a good reminder that we weren't the only ones headed somewhere because God asked.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        We landed, loaded into the vehicles, and the power at the airport went out right behind us.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The guest house had dinner waiting for us: fried chicken, pineapple, watermelon, plantain chips, salad, orange Fanta, and Coke. The showers had buckets and every bed had a mosquito net. That night I recorded a room tour for my boys. First thing I showed them: there was no handle on the door, so if I shut it, I was stuck.
      </p>

      <figure class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm not-prose">
        <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/welcome-dinner-guest-house.webp" alt="A table with fried chicken, plantain chips, watermelon, pineapple, salad, rolls, and cans of Coke and Fanta" class="w-full" />
        <figcaption class="p-4 bg-soho-white text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">The welcome dinner at the guest house</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        On the drive we passed rubber trees. I didn't know this before the trip, but rubber comes from tree sap. They carve a spiral into the bark and hang a bucket, kind of like tapping a maple tree for syrup, and white sap drips into it. Rubber is one of Liberia's biggest exports, and once you notice the trees you see them everywhere. We also had a giant bag of plantain chips for the road.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Somewhere along the way, Chris said something about how we hadn't been hit by anyone yet. Right after that, a little green car ran into us. There wasn't much damage, just a scratch on the bumper.
      </p>

      <figure class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm not-prose">
        <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/rubber-trees-roadside.webp" alt="Rows of rubber trees and palms beside a red dirt road" class="w-full" />
        <figcaption class="p-4 bg-soho-white text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">Rubber trees along the road</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">Turned Around at the Border</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The border crossing took a long time. Part of it was waiting in the car, and the rest was waiting for them to approve our passports. When we finally reached the last checkpoint, they told us they needed a call from the superintendent, who ranks above the governor. We only had a call from the governor.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        So after four to six hours of driving that day, we turned around and went back to Ganta to wait.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        We called a contact of Dennis Aggrey, who leads the <a href="https://crcaliberia.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-mcgennis-navy hover:text-mcgennis-gold transition-colors underline">Christian Revival Church Association (CRCA)</a>, and asked if he had room for sixteen travelers. While we were working out where to meet, we drove right past him on the road. We turned around and followed him to a missionary school of agriculture, where we unpacked for the night.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8 not-prose">
        <figure class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
          <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/school-of-agriculture-sign.webp" alt="A School of Agriculture sign made of sticks, under a porch with sculpted branches and potted plants" class="w-full h-72 object-cover" />
          <figcaption class="p-3 bg-soho-white text-xs text-gray-600 font-light">Where we ended up for the night</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
          <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/be-aware-of-bad-dog-sign.webp" alt="A hand-painted sign reading Be Aware of Bad Dog with a snarling dog" class="w-full h-72 object-cover" />
          <figcaption class="p-3 bg-soho-white text-xs text-gray-600 font-light">Duly noted</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Everyone ended up in the lounge, tired and pretty quiet. At some point I looked down and found a Liberian ten-dollar coin on the floor. It was the first Liberian money I'd seen. Then I dug my suitcase out of the truck so I could change and play two-on-two basketball with some of the locals.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Bill, the missionary who runs the school, made us dinner. You'd never guess what it was: Chicago-style deep dish pizza. Before we ate, he sang us his own version of a song: <em>&ldquo;I'm an international farmer, I'm an international harvester.&rdquo;</em> Bill is one of the most unique people I've ever met, and he was a fantastic host. It's always cool to see the different kinds of people God uses.
      </p>

      <figure class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm not-prose">
        <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/bill-kitchen-morning.webp" alt="Men talking across a wooden kitchen table with a basket of cucumbers" class="w-full" />
        <figcaption class="p-4 bg-soho-white text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">The kitchen at the School of Agriculture</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">Into Guinea</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The following morning we went to the border to see if things would work out and if our representative was there. The call came and we crossed. At Guinean border patrol everyone got their temperature checked, which took about ten or fifteen seconds each. Then Jeremy went in and didn't come out. Two minutes went by, then five. Ellen asked someone to pray, and right after the prayer Jeremy walked out. He had just gone to the bathroom.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        About an hour into Guinea, Jennifer started hinting that she needed a bathroom too, so I told Dennis I did. A couple minutes later we passed a hospital. We asked to use the bathroom, and afterward they asked us to stop on our way back so they could give us a tour.
      </p>

      <figure class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm not-prose">
        <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/guinea-hospital-courtyard.webp" alt="Our team and hospital staff in a brick courtyard between yellow buildings, with an Urgences sign and motorcycles" class="w-full" />
        <figcaption class="p-4 bg-soho-white text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">The hospital that let us stop</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        A little farther down the road we stopped at the government office that our escort worked for, and they did the same thing: come back through on your way home and see us again. I'll come back to why that stuck with me.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">Forty Trucks and Two Feet of Mud</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Then we hit a line of about forty trucks stopped on a dirt road. We drove up along the side to see what was going on. Two very large trucks had high-centered in the mud, and about thirty men were digging them out.
      </p>

      <figure class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm not-prose">
        <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/mud-road-stuck-truck.webp" alt="A crowd of men standing on a torn-up red dirt road beside a stuck, heavily loaded truck in the jungle" class="w-full" />
        <figcaption class="p-4 bg-soho-white text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">The hold-up</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Every ten minutes or so a group of motorcycles would come through and try to squeeze past. They'd dug the road down about two feet, so every motorcycle had to be lifted onto the higher ground by hand. It took two or three guys each time. I helped with one and got a little rookie rash from the rim. They had made it look so easy.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Once the two trucks were finally out, the next driver in line went for it and got stuck too.
      </p>

      <figure class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm not-prose">
        <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/mud-road-digging-out.webp" alt="Men digging around a mud-covered truck with shovels while others watch" class="w-full" />
        <figcaption class="p-4 bg-soho-white text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">Digging out, by hand</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        By then word had gotten around about who we were and why we were there, and the people decided to get us through first. They let us cut the line and carved a ramp into the two-foot drop so we could drive down safely. Not every vehicle had left enough room for the big trucks, so they had those drivers back up and move over to make space for us.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The overwhelming selflessness to help us was truly humbling.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        People celebrate wins differently there, too. When a truck gets pulled out, everybody celebrates together. Everyone is a winner.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">Watching for God to Work</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Our first night at the hotel in Guinea we ate black snapper. At devotion that night, Ellen gave us an assignment for the week:
      </p>

      <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-mcgennis-gold pl-6 my-8 italic text-gray-700">
        &ldquo;God has called us here this week, so he wouldn't call us here to do nothing with us. So we know he has a plan and he is going to work. My assignment to you guys all this week is to be watching for how God is working. And I'm gonna ask every day, how did you see God work today? And some days it's gonna be, well, we made it. We're not sleeping on the side of the road tonight.&rdquo;
      </blockquote>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        On Wednesday I accidentally woke up an hour early, so I used the time to write that night's Bible study on Malachi 3. Breakfast was scrambled eggs with onions, bread, papaya, oatmeal, and black coffee. I also used some of that quiet time to design something for my boys: when I die, each of my sons will get a Bible, and that Bible will hold their inheritance.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        We heard the locals wouldn't be available until 3 p.m., so we waited. Then we practiced the stories. The idea is simple: tell a Bible story, talk through some questions about it, and then have the listeners learn to tell it themselves. Once it's recorded in their own language, the recordings and the speakers we brought can play the story over and over without a translator.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8 not-prose">
        <figure class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
          <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/practicing-bible-stories.webp" alt="Two team members in yellow chairs on a porch practicing a story from printed pages" class="w-full h-72 object-cover" />
          <figcaption class="p-3 bg-soho-white text-xs text-gray-600 font-light">Practicing the stories</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
          <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/pant-legs-at-ankles.webp" alt="A man on a porch bench laughing, with the zip-off legs of his pants bunched around his ankles" class="w-full h-72 object-cover" />
          <figcaption class="p-3 bg-soho-white text-xs text-gray-600 font-light">The pant legs</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        While we were practicing, Chris unzipped his pant legs and let them drop to his ankles, which happened to distract Shane.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        After recording for a while, we were told to pack up because we were switching hotels. We didn't know it until later, but the new place was rougher and worse for recording. Everyone kept moving on with good attitudes. With the move, we got to church an hour and a half late.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        I wandered around getting as many photos and videos as I could, which was hard in that space. Jennifer introduced herself and the team. They used a little demonstration with Jeremy to show the difference between a disciple and a maker of disciples: Jeremy follows Jesus and tells people about Jesus, but he doesn't stop there. He teaches other people how to make disciples too.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Then we told the story from Mark 5 of the man possessed by many evil spirits, the one who couldn't be held by chains and ran out from the tombs to meet Jesus. We went through the questions, and then asked the people to be brave and try telling the story themselves. They did. One man summed it up: when we receive the gospel, we shall not stay where we are. We go out and teach other people.
      </p>

      <figure class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm not-prose">
        <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/wednesday-story-session.webp" alt="A team member in a cap speaking into a microphone under colorful bunting" class="w-full" />
        <figcaption class="p-4 bg-soho-white text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">Wednesday night at the church</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        While I was outside I got to talk to Charles about video games, and he helped me plan a new video game event for <a href="https://sportscrusaders.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-mcgennis-navy hover:text-mcgennis-gold transition-colors underline">Sports Crusaders</a> over a Friday, Saturday, and Sunday, coming in 2027 or 2028.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">The First Stories in Konon</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The next morning everyone was ready, and we got started recording right away. I was assigned the Konon language (pronounced &ldquo;Kono&rdquo;). It took a little while to get started because we had to wait for the translator. Wilson was helping me, and Amos Bamba was recording the stories.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Wilson told me there was no Christian material in Konon yet. When he reached out to Amos, Amos was in the middle of hosting a conference at his church. He told the conference, &ldquo;I have to go do this. The conference can wait.&rdquo;
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        During a break Wilson told me about the organization he's part of, <a href="https://ttiglobal.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-mcgennis-navy hover:text-mcgennis-gold transition-colors underline">TTI</a>. They've planted more than 171 churches in the last two or three years.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        That day, God chose me to be the guy recording what we believed was the first Christian material in Konon. I feel so honored to be the guy God chose for this. I later found out there's actually an audio New Testament already out there, so we weren't the very first. I'm still grateful I got to be part of it.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8 not-prose">
        <figure class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
          <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/recording-room.webp" alt="A storyteller reading from printed pages across a coffee table with a boom microphone, laptop, and recorder" class="w-full h-72 object-cover" />
          <figcaption class="p-3 bg-soho-white text-xs text-gray-600 font-light">A recording session</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
          <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/zoom-recorder-and-log.webp" alt="A Zoom H4n Pro recorder mid-recording next to a handwritten log of story file names" class="w-full h-72 object-cover object-top" />
          <figcaption class="p-3 bg-soho-white text-xs text-gray-600 font-light">The recorder and the log, one story per file</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Recording works one story at a time. The storyteller tells a story in Konon, the recorder captures it as its own file, and I write the file number and story name in my notebook so nothing gets lost. My notes are mostly logistics, like &ldquo;started 15 seconds in&rdquo; or &ldquo;Wilson walked in,&rdquo; so I'd know what to fix later.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The stories run in order through the Bible. On Thursday they went from King David and Nathan's story into the life of Jesus: His birth, the triumphal entry, the Last Supper, His arrest and trial, and Jesus alive again. From there we went into Acts: the promise of God's Spirit, Peter's sermon, Peter and the crippled beggar, the lying couple, Stephen, the African man, and Paul meeting Jesus.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8 not-prose">
        <figure class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
          <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/konon-files-and-notebook.webp" alt="A laptop showing folders of Konon story audio files behind an open handwritten log" class="w-full h-72 object-cover object-top" />
          <figcaption class="p-3 bg-soho-white text-xs text-gray-600 font-light">Thursday night: matching every file to its story</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
          <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/konon-recording-log.webp" alt="A handwritten log of numbered Acts stories with their audio file names" class="w-full h-72 object-cover object-top" />
          <figcaption class="p-3 bg-soho-white text-xs text-gray-600 font-light">The log, stories 030 through 047</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        That night I sat with my laptop and notebook and matched every file to its story. By the end of Thursday the log was up to story 029.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Then we kept going through Acts: Ananias obeys God, Peter and Cornelius, the church in Antioch, God freeing Peter from jail, Paul and Barnabas mistaken for gods, Paul's vision, the Philippian jailer, Paul in Athens, Priscilla and Aquila, Apollos, Eutychus, Paul's goodbye in Ephesus, and the plot to kill Paul and his escape to Caesarea. By the end, the log ran past story 047.
      </p>

      <figure class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm not-prose">
        <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/nzerekore-rooftop-view.webp" alt="A rooftop view over red tin roofs onto a dirt street full of motorcycles" class="w-full" />
        <figcaption class="p-4 bg-soho-white text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">The view from the new hotel</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">Sunday</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        On Sunday we went back to the church we had invested in all week. Half our group went with Jeremy, who had been asked to preach at another church. I really wanted to hear him, but Wilson asked me to stay, so I stayed.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Everyone was dancing and singing, and you couldn't hear anything except the drums. Joseph was killing it with the dance moves. Then Shane got to preach. He shared his testimony and talked about how obedience comes from trusting God's character.
      </p>

      <figure class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm not-prose">
        <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/sunday-preaching-translator.webp" alt="A preacher and a translator at two blue-draped pulpits beneath colorful bunting" class="w-full" />
        <figcaption class="p-4 bg-soho-white text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">Sunday morning, preaching through a translator</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        When we got back, we finished work on the recordings and loaded them onto <a href="https://megavoice.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-mcgennis-navy hover:text-mcgennis-gold transition-colors underline">MegaVoice</a> players, small solar-powered audio players that can play the stories without electricity or internet.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">The Road Home</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        At 5 a.m. Monday we left Guinea and headed back to Liberia. About thirty minutes in, we heard honks from a motorcycle trying to get us to stop. It was the French guy who had been helping us translate. We'd left MegaVoice players at the hotel for him, so he went back to get them and planned to maybe join us later.
      </p>

      <figure class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm not-prose">
        <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/foggy-red-road-guinea.webp" alt="A red dirt road disappearing into morning fog between palm trees" class="w-full" />
        <figcaption class="p-4 bg-soho-white text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">Early on the drive home from Guinea</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm not-prose">
        <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/van-in-mud-way-home.webp" alt="The back of a white van sunk into deep red mud ruts on a jungle road" class="w-full" />
        <figcaption class="p-4 bg-soho-white text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">Back through the mud on the way home</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Later we got stuck behind a car with an LED sign in the back that said <strong>GOD'S PLAN</strong>. So for a while we were, quite literally, following God's plan.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        We made it back to Aggrey Cove, the guest house where we first stayed. Everyone got to eat some African pizza, a really big one, and we got to chat with the missionaries staying next to us. I took a call with Leo and Steph, and they got to meet Charles. We'd told Leo that Charles was like the Hulk, big and strong. Leo said, &ldquo;Charles isn't green!&rdquo;
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        I'd been sick the last couple of days, with something I think I picked up before I got to Africa. I'd been missing my family so much those last few days.
      </p>

      <figure class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm not-prose">
        <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/liberia/leo-and-brother-at-home.webp" alt="Leo in a red shirt hugging his baby brother on the couch at home" class="w-full" />
