      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">The Great Commission, Up Close</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Before Jesus went back to the Father, he gave his followers one job:
      </p>

      <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-mcgennis-gold pl-6 my-8 italic text-gray-700">
        &ldquo;All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me. Go therefore and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, teaching them to observe all that I have commanded you. And behold, I am with you always, to the end of the age.&rdquo;
        <span class="block not-italic text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2">Matthew 28:18&ndash;20 (ESV)</span>
      </blockquote>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The command is to make disciples. Going is how it starts, and teaching is how it keeps going. That's the same difference the demonstration with Jeremy showed on Wednesday night: a disciple follows Jesus, and a disciple-maker teaches someone else to follow Him and then to teach others.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        That's why the stories are built the way they are. We don't tell a story and leave. We ask questions, and then the listeners tell it back until they can tell it to someone else. The man at the church on Wednesday put it best: when we receive the gospel, we shall not stay where we are.
      </p>

      <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-mcgennis-gold pl-6 my-8 italic text-gray-700">
        &ldquo;How then will they call on him in whom they have not believed? And how are they to believe in him of whom they have never heard? And how are they to hear without someone preaching?&rdquo;
        <span class="block not-italic text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2">Romans 10:14 (ESV)</span>
      </blockquote>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Recording stories in Konon is one small answer to that question. A solar-powered player doesn't need a translator, electricity, or us. It keeps telling the story after we leave.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        It fits that so many of the stories I recorded came from Acts. Acts is the Great Commission happening: a church in Antioch, a jailer in Philippi, Paul in Athens, the gospel moving from one town to the next through ordinary people.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        And the people carrying it now are local. Wilson's network has planted more than 171 churches in the last two or three years. Amos put his own conference on hold to record. CRCA describes its mission as working &ldquo;to establish, strengthen, and mobilize churches across Liberia, Sierra Leone, and Guinea to fulfill the Great Commission.&rdquo; We were guests in work that was already happening before we landed and will keep happening after we left.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">What I'm Bringing Home</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        I brought home a wooden lion for Leo and a cloth for Steph to use. I also came home ten pounds lighter. The rest of what I'm bringing home is harder to pack.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        America is so different from the rest of the world. There is a selfishness that has fallen over the eyes of its people.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        On our way to Guinea we saw a great picture of Luke 10, where Jesus sends out the seventy-two. They go from town to town, and when they find a &ldquo;son of peace&rdquo; who welcomes them, they stay there. If a town won't receive them, they move on. We were shown that kind of welcome over and over. Dennis's contact took in sixteen strangers. A hospital let us use the bathroom when we asked. The people on a mud road let us go first. The hospital and the government office both asked us to come back through on our way home.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        We should always open our doors to the people who need it, and invite them to come back on the way home.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        I also noticed how stories travel there. Everywhere we went, people asked to hear about everything we'd done.
      </p>

